\description{
Yearly latent trajectories (\eqn{\theta}) of the level of bilateral
relations for every country pair with at least one recorded event,
estimated with bilatr from GDELT 1.0 event data. The data are not
shipped with the package: \code{\link[=download_bilatr_theta]{download_bilatr_theta()}} downloads them from a
versioned data release (see "Getting the data"). A small extract,
\link{theta_sample}, ships for examples.
}
\details{
There are two tables. \code{download_bilatr_theta("directed")} holds the directed
model (one series per sender-receiver pair); \code{download_bilatr_theta("undirected")}
holds the undirected model (one series per unordered pair, events in
both directions pooled). Each row gives both the smoothed estimate --
the posterior of \eqn{\theta_{dt}} given all years, 1991-2025 -- and the
forward-filtered estimate, which uses only events up to and including
year \eqn{t} and so is the quantity to use when no look-ahead is allowed
(e.g. as a predictor in forecasting).

\strong{Higher values of \eqn{\theta} mean more cooperative relations.} The
reference category is \code{ModifiedRootCode} 5, "Consult: meet, discuss, or
visit", a cooperative class, and \eqn{\theta} is oriented so that higher
values make the reference category more likely relative to the other
action types (see the \code{reference_category} argument of
\code{\link[=prepare_stan_data]{prepare_stan_data()}}). The sign is fixed by the model's orientation
constraint (\eqn{\alpha_1 \ge 0} for the reference class), the location
by the \eqn{\theta_0} prior (mean pinned at 0 across dyads) and the scale
by normalizing the discrimination parameters \eqn{\alpha} to
root-mean-square 1. Because the \code{stable_gamma} model includes
country-level category offsets, \eqn{\theta} is measured net of each
country's baseline mix of event types.

Dyad-years before a dyad's first recorded event, and dyad-years in which
either country was not a member of the state system, are dropped: the
likelihood is only evaluated in years with events, so \eqn{\theta}
before the first event is the random-walk prior smoothed backwards
from that event and carries no information from the dyad's own data,
and a year outside membership has no events by construction (see
\code{\link[=aggregate_events]{aggregate_events()}}, "Actor periods"). So dyads with Czechoslovakia
end in 1992, and dyads with Czechia or Slovakia start in 1993 at the
earliest. After the first event every year is kept, including years
without events (where \eqn{\theta} is interpolated or, at the end of a
series, extrapolated by the random walk), so each dyad's series has no
gaps and ends in 2025, or in 1992 for dyads with Czechoslovakia. All
numeric columns are rounded to 4 decimal places.

\strong{Events.} GDELT 1.0 events (the historical backfiles and the daily
updates) dated 1991-01-01 through 2025-12-31, accessed 2026-01-31,
read with
\code{read_gdelt(actor_types = c("GOV", "MIL", "SPY"), actor_type_match = "both", cross_border = TRUE, recover_country_codes = TRUE, states_only = TRUE)} (the replication package's \verb{00b_build_events.R}). An event is
kept when both actors carry \code{GOV}, \code{MIL} or \code{SPY} in any of their
\code{Type1Code}/\code{Type2Code}/\code{Type3Code} fields and the two actors' country
codes differ; an empty actor country code is first filled from the
actor code's prefix (\code{recover_country_codes}). \code{states_only} then keeps
only events in which both countries are members of the Correlates of
War state system (v2024) in the year of the event, and rewrites their
codes to ISO3 (see \code{\link[=filter_gdelt_states]{filter_gdelt_states()}}), which removes GDELT's
regional and other non-state codes. All events are used, root events or
not, except that an explicit filter on the event code drops events with
CAMEO codes 010-015, 017, 024x, 025x, 040 and 041 (a Goldstein score
between -1 and 1; \code{BilatrClass} 0 in the original coding: neutral and
low-intensity statements), which is why two \code{ModifiedRootCode} classes
do not occur, and events with non-CAMEO codes.

\strong{Model.} The \code{stable_gamma} model, at yearly resolution, with events
grouped by \code{ModifiedRootCode} (16 of its 18 classes occur in the data,
so \eqn{A = 16}), reference category 5 ("Consult: meet, discuss, or
visit"), \code{min_n_events = 1}, and 8 chains of 1,000 warmup and 1,000
sampling iterations each. The Stan program and the data preparation
used are the ones in this version of bilatr.
}
\section{Getting the data}{

\code{download_bilatr_theta("directed")} and
\code{download_bilatr_theta("undirected")} download a table once, verify its
checksum, and cache it in \code{tools::R_user_dir("bilatr", "data")}; later
calls read the cache without using the internet.
The data are published as versioned releases of
\url{https://github.com/doktorandahl/bilatr-data} under the CC BY 4.0
license, archived on Zenodo. Each bilatr release is pinned to one data
version (\code{v2026.11.1} for this one), so the same code returns the same
data; \code{download_bilatr_theta(version = "latest")} asks for the newest
release, and \code{\link[=bilatr_theta_versions]{bilatr_theta_versions()}} lists them. \code{\link[=bilatr_clear_data_cache]{bilatr_clear_data_cache()}}
deletes the cache.

Without bilatr, the same files can be read directly, e.g.
\code{readRDS(url(".../releases/download/v2026.11.1/theta_directed.rds"))}, or
\code{read.csv()} on \code{theta_directed.csv.gz}; see the data repository's
README.

Please cite the data release by the DOI of the version you use, for
these data (\code{v2026.11.1}) \doi{10.5281/zenodo.24109618}; the DOI
\doi{10.5281/zenodo.23951818} refers to all versions of the data
release. Please also cite the GDELT references below. Cite the bilatr
package (\code{citation("bilatr")}) for the method and the software; the
entry for the data release is there too.

The earlier data version \code{v2026.11} (\doi{10.5281/zenodo.23951819}) is
superseded. It lacked six states because of a country-code crosswalk
error, and is still available with
\code{download_bilatr_theta(version = "v2026.11")} for reproducing earlier
work only.
}

\section{Known gaps}{

\itemize{
\item \strong{Four absent states.} Slovenia, Bosnia and Herzegovina, Kosovo and
Montenegro are Correlates of War states but are absent from both
tables: GDELT 1.0 has no country code for them, so once the actor
filter is applied it has no events with their government actors as
source or target. Their absence reflects the coverage of the data
source, not an absence of interaction, and the tables say nothing
about their bilateral relations.
\item \strong{Missing and incomplete days.} GDELT provides no daily event files
for 24 days, most of them 14 June-1 July 2025, and for a further 13
days in 1991-1995 the files contain events, but none that pass the
filters. Because events are aggregated to dyad-years, these gaps
reduce the number of events in the affected years but do not create
missing dyad-years. The model conditions on the number of events and
measures relations from their composition, so a gap reduces the
precision of the estimates for the affected years rather than
shifting their level, provided the missing days are not
systematically different from the rest of the year. The 2025 gap is
the exception: it coincides with the war between Israel and Iran in
June 2025, so the 2025 estimates for the dyads involved in that
conflict rest on a composition of events from which the war itself is
largely missing.
}
}

